## Code Example
```javascript
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';
import { NativeEventEmitter, NativeModules } from 'react-native';

export function useLiveList(channel) {
  const [updates, setUpdates] = useState([]);
  const buf = useRef([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const emitter = new NativeEventEmitter(NativeModules[channel]);
    const subscription = emitter.addListener('dataUpdate', (data) => {
      buf.current.push(data);
    });

    const flushInterval = setInterval(() => {
      if (buf.current.length > 0) {
        setUpdates(prev => [...prev, ...buf.current]);
        buf.current = [];
      }
    }, 16); // ~60fps

    return () => {
      subscription.remove();
      clearInterval(flushInterval);
    };
  }, [channel]);

  return updates;
}

// Usage in component
function LiveDashboard() {
  const data = useLiveList('LiveDataSource');

  const renderItem = useCallback(({ item }) => (
    <MemoizedListItem item={item} />
  ), []);

  return (
    <FlatList
      data={data}
      renderItem={renderItem}
      getItemLayout={(data, index) => ({ length: 80, offset: 80 * index, index })}
      initialNumToRender={10}
      maxToRenderPerBatch={5}
      windowSize={10}
      removeClippedSubviews
      keyExtractor={(item, index) => `item-${item.id || index}`}
    />
  );
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests offline-first sync design, conflict resolution, and native bridge considerations. Evaluates real-world trade-offs between consistency models and performance.

## Key Concepts

- **Offline-first sync architecture**: Prioritizes local-first writes with eventual consistency to remote
- **TurboModule/JSI bridges**: Enables synchronous, type-safe native communication without bridge serialization overhead
- **Local SQLite queue**: Tracks pending changes with timestamps and operation types for reliable sync
- **Conflict resolution strategies**: Version vectors for causality tracking; CRDT or last-write-wins for merge semantics
- **Schema migrations**: Additive migrations with backward compatibility; migration scripts stored in versioned history table
- **Background workers**: Dedicated threads prevent UI jank during heavy I/O operations
- **Backpressure handling**: Bounded queue sizes with configurable flush thresholds

